 # Living Survey: cancer                                                                                             
                                                                                                                     
 ## Introduction                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
 This document collects the scientific literature regarding **cancer**.                                              
+                                                                                                                    
+## Computational Diagnostics & Imaging                                                                              
+                                                                                                                    
+Recent advances in medical imaging and spatial transcriptomics are significantly enhancing early detection and      
tissue characterization. Active learning strategies for spatial transcriptomics have been benchmarked against uniform
sampling, demonstrating that uncertainty-based and diversity-driven selection can optimize cost-efficiency in        
capturing morphologically distinct regions for breast and cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas [^2609.27208]. In       
parallel, microbiome-based detection frameworks like BreCol provide rigorous benchmarks across multiple cancer types,
highlighting the persistent challenge of generalizing classical and deep-learning models across temporal and cohort  
boundaries [^2609.27207]. Foundation models are also being adapted for mammography; embeddings from domain-specific  
and general medical imaging models can capture longitudinal pre-diagnostic tissue changes, with clinically grounded  
pretraining proving essential for detecting subtle malignant drifts [^2609.26605]. To address post-treatment         
monitoring, Mammo-LIFE introduces a multimodal framework that fuses longitudinal mammographic features with clinical 
variables via low-rank adaptation and view-attention pooling to predict radiotherapy outcomes [^2609.26443]. Beyond  
imaging, non-invasive diagnostic modalities are expanding: terahertz spectroscopy of urine vapors shows promise in   
distinguishing prostate cancer from benign conditions [^2609.23814], while whole-slide pathology foundation models   
like WILSON enable patient-level multi-magnification analysis that surpasses dedicated case-level models in          
diagnostic text retrieval and generation [^2609.25123]. Explainability remains critical for clinical adoption; formal
concept analysis-guided counterfactual explanations have demonstrated perfect validity and emergent sparsity for     
multi-modal breast cancer diagnosis, offering actionable insights where traditional attribution methods fail         
[^2609.20067]. Furthermore, standardized datasets like ERCPMP-Gx are bridging the gap between endoscopic phenotypes, 
histopathology, and germline genetics to support AI-driven classification of hereditary polyposis syndromes          
[^2609.20815]. Liquid biopsy techniques are also maturing, with cell-free DNA nucleosome positioning profiling       
establishing robust, repeat-based signatures that discriminate healthy individuals from patients across multiple     
cancer types [^10.5526_err-00042900]. Complementary proteomic approaches, such as urinary proteomics in breast cancer
cohorts, continue to identify novel biomarker candidates for non-invasive screening [^10.5281_zenodo.20393677].      
+                                                                                                                    
+## Tumor Evolution & Microenvironment                                                                               
+                                                                                                                    
+Understanding the physical and evolutionary dynamics of tumor progression is crucial for predicting invasion and    
metastasis. Research into intratumor heterogeneity reveals that the order of driver mutation acquisition             
fundamentally shapes clonal structure; threshold-like fitness effects and order-dependent terminal fitness can drive 
highly concentrated malignant populations or cause faster-growing pathways to dominate asymptotically [^2609.25838]. 
The tumor microenvironment also plays a mechanical role in progression: tumor-induced lipid loss in adipocytes       
physically remodels white adipose tissue, accelerating breast cancer invasion under conditions of elevated tissue    
pressure, a phenomenon exacerbated by obesity [^2609.23229]. Mechanistic modeling of tumor growth is advancing       
through 2D reaction-diffusion frameworks that estimate cell diffusivity and proliferation rates from single-point    
biopsies, providing theoretical validation for dispersion length and front velocity as risk-stratification biomarkers
[^2609.22363]. At the metastatic front, single-cell and spatial multi-omics are uncovering confined tumor            
microenvironments in clear cell renal cell carcinoma lung metastases, characterized by cancer-associated fibroblast  
lymphocyte exclusion sites [^10.5281_zenodo.18767278]. Similarly, pan-cancer brain metastasis atlases are being      
constructed to map spatially resolved cellular states across diverse primary origins [^10.5281_zenodo.21816284].     
Oncogenic chromatin remodeling further drives metastasis; the DEK oncoprotein functions as a context-dependent       
transcriptional regulator in melanoma, with its binding landscape and phosphorylation status directly influencing    
chromatin compaction and tumor progression [^10.17638_03194520].                                                     
+                                                                                                                    
+## Therapeutic Optimization & Immunotherapy                                                                         
+                                                                                                                    
+Translating computational insights into clinical treatment strategies requires robust frameworks for personalized   
therapy and toxicity management. Federated learning architectures are being deployed to optimize pancreatic cancer   
treatment across distributed sites, overcoming data privacy barriers while handling partially overlapping local      
features [^2609.24718]. Bridging the gap between trial data and real-world practice, transportability methods combine
routine care data with trial summary measures to estimate absolute treatment effects, as demonstrated in breast      
cancer endocrine therapy outcomes [^2609.24305]. In radiation oncology, uncertainty-quantified ensemble models       
predict severe radiotherapy-induced immunosuppression in esophageal cancer, enabling risk-aware proton therapy       
selection and dose optimization [^2609.25139]. Quantum-classical hybrid attention mechanisms are being explored for  
histopathology-based molecular profiling, showing selective gains in data-limited cancer cohorts by redistributing   
predictive accuracy toward prognostically relevant biological targets [^2609.21115]. Immunotherapy development       
benefits from hybrid quantum-classical neural networks that improve sample efficiency in peptide-HLA binding         
prediction, a critical step for neoantigen identification in personalized cancer vaccines [^2609.19642]. Metabolic   
interventions are also yielding novel therapeutic avenues; omega-3 fatty acid interventions induce mitochondrial     
succinylation networks that disrupt homeostasis and trigger ferroptosis in prostate cancer cells                     
[^10.17638_03195029]. Signaling pathway modulation remains a key target, with deubiquitinating enzymes like USP42    
identified as critical regulators of YAP/TAZ activity in mesothelioma, offering potential therapeutic entry points   
[^10.17638_03193849]. Nutritional strategies are being re-evaluated through lipidomics, revealing that               
polyunsaturated fatty acids mediate the antitumor effects of fasting by inducing lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis  
in triple-negative breast cancer [^10.21954_ou.ro.00108036]. Finally, targeted small-molecule therapies continue to  
evolve, with non-genotoxic topoisomerase II catalytic inhibitors being characterized for their potential to bypass   
conventional resistance mechanisms [^10.14288_1.0451818].                                                            
+                                                                                                                    
+## Multi-Omics & Molecular Profiling                                                                                
+                                                                                                                    
+High-dimensional data integration and advanced statistical modeling are driving discoveries in cancer genomics and  
transcriptomics. Non-parametric estimators for excess recurrent events are being adapted to cancer cohorts, combining
patient-level data with population statistics to quantify disease burden in rectal cancer [^2609.26328]. Variable    
selection in ultra-high-dimensional genomic data is improved by structured screen-and-select frameworks that leverage
predictor correlations, demonstrating enhanced external discrimination in ovarian cancer prediction tasks            
[^2609.24945]. At the proteome level, ridge regression-based feature selection identifies stable transcriptomic      
predictors for thousands of proteins across cancer cell lines, revealing globally predictive and context-specific    
gene modules [^2609.24004]. Bayesian inference for mixed graphical models is being applied to heterogeneous data,    
successfully recovering meaningful gene expression dependencies and subtype relationships in breast cancer datasets  
[^2609.23857]. Regulatory networks are being decoded through inferred hub-miRNAs, which provide biologically informed
recoding of transcriptomic data that consistently improves drug mechanism-of-action modeling [^2609.21280].          
Pan-cancer co-expression networks are mapping hub genes that show strong potential for predicting differential drug  
responses across malignancies [^10.5281_zenodo.2574643]. Epigenetic profiling continues to yield diagnostic insights,
with nucleosome repositioning landscapes in glioblastoma cell-free DNA offering potential biomarkers for non-invasive
detection [^10.5526_err-00042862]. Multi-omic prognostic modeling frameworks are dissecting tumor-immune ecosystems  
across thousands of checkpoint blockade treatment cases to identify cellular programs predictive of therapeutic      
response [^10.5281_zenodo.18706180]. Finally, large-scale RNA-seq analyses in specific cohorts, such as Korean       
colorectal cancer populations, continue to refine molecular subtype classifications and highlight dysregulated       
metabolic and cell-cycle pathways [^10.5281_zenodo.8307739].                                                         
                                                                                                                     

