The tumor microenvironment also plays a mechanical role in progression: tumor-induced lipid loss in adipocytes       
physically remodels white adipose tissue, accelerating breast cancer invasion under conditions of elevated tissue    
pressure, a phenomenon exacerbated by obesity [^2609.23229]. Mechanistic modeling of tumor growth is advancing       
through 2D reaction-diffusion frameworks that estimate cell diffusivity and proliferation rates from single-point    
biopsies, providing theoretical validation for dispersion length and front velocity as risk-stratification biomarkers
[^2609.22363]. At the metastatic front, single-cell and spatial multi-omics are uncovering confined tumor            
microenvironments in clear cell renal cell carcinoma lung metastases, characterized by cancer-associated fibroblast  
lymphocyte exclusion sites [^10.5281_zenodo.18767278]. Similarly, pan-cancer brain metastasis atlases are being      
constructed to map spatially resolved cellular states across diverse primary origins [^10.5281_zenodo.21816284].     
Oncogenic chromatin remodeling further drives metastasis; the DEK oncoprotein functions as a context-dependent       
transcriptional regulator in melanoma, with its binding landscape and phosphorylation status directly influencing    
chromatin compaction and tumor progression [^10.17638_03194520].                                                     
