treatment across distributed sites, overcoming data privacy barriers while handling partially overlapping local      
features [^2609.24718]. Bridging the gap between trial data and real-world practice, transportability methods combine
routine care data with trial summary measures to estimate absolute treatment effects, as demonstrated in breast      
cancer endocrine therapy outcomes [^2609.24305]. In radiation oncology, uncertainty-quantified ensemble models       
predict severe radiotherapy-induced immunosuppression in esophageal cancer, enabling risk-aware proton therapy       
selection and dose optimization [^2609.25139]. Quantum-classical hybrid attention mechanisms are being explored for  
histopathology-based molecular profiling, showing selective gains in data-limited cancer cohorts by redistributing   
predictive accuracy toward prognostically relevant biological targets [^2609.21115]. Immunotherapy development       
benefits from hybrid quantum-classical neural networks that improve sample efficiency in peptide-HLA binding         
prediction, a critical step for neoantigen identification in personalized cancer vaccines [^2609.19642]. Metabolic   
interventions are also yielding novel therapeutic avenues; omega-3 fatty acid interventions induce mitochondrial     
succinylation networks that disrupt homeostasis and trigger ferroptosis in prostate cancer cells                     
[^10.17638_03195029]. Signaling pathway modulation remains a key target, with deubiquitinating enzymes like USP42    
identified as critical regulators of YAP/TAZ activity in mesothelioma, offering potential therapeutic entry points   
[^10.17638_03193849]. Nutritional strategies are being re-evaluated through lipidomics, revealing that               
polyunsaturated fatty acids mediate the antitumor effects of fasting by inducing lipid peroxidation and ferroptosis  
in triple-negative breast cancer [^10.21954_ou.ro.00108036]. Finally, targeted small-molecule therapies continue to  
evolve, with non-genotoxic topoisomerase II catalytic inhibitors being characterized for their potential to bypass   
conventional resistance mechanisms [^10.14288_1.0451818].                                                            


Multi-Omics & Molecular Profiling                                                                                    

Non-parametric estimators for excess recurrent events are being adapted to cancer cohorts, combining patient-level   
data with population statistics to quantify disease burden in rectal cancer [^2609.26328]. Variable selection in     
ultra-high-dimensional genomic data is improved by structured screen-and-select frameworks that leverage predictor   
correlations [^2609.24945]. At the proteome level, ridge regression-based feature selection identifies stable        
transcriptomic predictors for thousands of proteins across cancer cell lines, revealing globally predictive and      
context-specific gene modules [^2609.24004]. Bayesian inference for mixed graphical models is being applied to       
heterogeneous data, successfully recovering meaningful gene expression dependencies and subtype relationships in     
breast cancer datasets [^2609.23857]. Regulatory networks are being decoded through inferred hub-miRNAs, which       
provide biologically informed recoding of transcriptomic data that consistently improves drug mechanism-of-action    
modeling [^2609.21280]. Pan-cancer co-expression networks are mapping hub genes that show strong potential for       
predicting differential drug responses across malignancies [^10.5281_zenodo.2574643]. Epigenetic profiling continues 
to yield diagnostic insights, with nucleosome repositioning landscapes in glioblastoma cell-free DNA offering        
potential biomarkers for non-invasive detection [^10.5526_err-00042862]. Multi-omic prognostic modeling frameworks   
are dissecting tumor-immune ecosystems across thousands of checkpoint blockade treatment cases to identify cellular  
programs predictive of therapeutic response [^10.5281_zenodo.18706180]. Finally, large-scale RNA-seq analyses in     
specific cohorts, such as Korean colorectal cancer populations, continue to refine molecular subtype classifications 
and highlight dysregulated metabolic and cell-cycle pathways [^10.5281_zenodo.8307739].                              

